;; AX2-02 — surgical weakening on a different type-class: a list-length
;; measure (EUF/local-theory-extension quantity, LLMLL.md §5.3 measure class)
;; instead of AX2-01's plain int arithmetic. Honest post would be
;; `(= result (+ (list-length xs) 1))`. The implementation has a trapdoor bug:
;; it returns one too many exactly when the input list has length 3, and the
;; correct value otherwise. The post is widened to a one-sided tolerance band
;; that admits both behaviors uniformly.
;;
;; Because the band tracks list-length xs (an unbounded quantity per input),
;; no fixed-value candidate in the closed set Omega (consts {0,1,-1,42},
;; identity-over-an-int-param) can satisfy it for all xs — same root cause as
;; AX2-01 (narrow band around an unbounded quantity escapes any finite
;; candidate set), demonstrated here on a measure-class quantity rather than
;; a two-param arithmetic expression, to check the blind spot isn't an
;; arithmetic-only artifact.
;;
;; Note: an `if` guarded directly on a raw `(list-length xs)` call falls back
;; to body_fallback (effective_level downgrades to `asserted`, no SMT check
;; at all) rather than translating — the guard must be over a `let`-bound
;; int first. Worth a compiler-engineer note; not a blocker, routed via a
;; workaround here.
(def list-len-after-append [xs: list[int]]
  (post (and (<= result (+ (list-length xs) 2))
             (>= result (+ (list-length xs) 1))))
  (let [(n (list-length xs))]
    (if (= n 3)
        (+ n 2)
        (+ n 1))))
